horizontal space aligns columns. nothing else — never to centre a line,
never to pad one out. end-user content only; in code it churns the diff.

the test: do you ever read DOWN the second column? if not, alignment is
only a hanging indent.

  1  a cell wraps         -> not a table. hanging indent, no delimiter.
  2  nothing wraps AND
     you read down a col  -> table. delimit with |, generate with
                             column -t -s'|' -o' | ' | sed 's/ | $//'.
                             never hand-space.
  3  anything else        -> hanging indent. one offset per block, two
                             spaces past the longest key, four if three
                             or more columns.

quoted output is verbatim, never reformatted. a printed grid is a table
by the test, but a | you added is a lie about what ran.

column -t is single-line, so a wrapped cell cannot be regenerated by any
command: that block is hand-maintained, and it drifts on the next edit.

no trailing | on an empty last cell. column pads one — that is the sed.
